gregs@polari.online.com writes:
>I hope they devise a new magic system in the next version. 

Me too!  My roommate and I devised our own magic system which we like
very much.  It creates a real distinction between the magic using classes
and the fighter ones.  Some of the main features are:

1)  Priests and Wizards had their INT and WIS jacked up slightly.
    (To facilitate memorizing spells.)
2)  Spell points are generated much much slower.
    Techincally, if ((u.uen<u.uenmax) && (!(moves%(60-ACURR(A_INT))))) {
		u.uen += rn2((int)ACURR(A_WIS)/5 + 1) + 1; ...
3)  Spell points only advance every other level gained. (So 1/2 as fast.) 
4)  Spells cost much much more in spell points.
    Technically, (spellev(i) * spellev(i) + 7 - spellev(i)))
5)  Spell books are much easier to read and memorize.
6)  Once a spell is memorized, it is memorized forever.  (Or until you see
    that Maude person again. :-) )
7)  Certain spells like polymorph and extra healing have been made higher
    level to avoid abuse.

In our games, idiot fighters will almost never be able to cast big spells
like "finger of death."  (Unless they are level 20 or have quaffed a bunch
of gain energy potions.)  Wizards and priests regenerate spell points more
quickly (mostly due to their high INT and WIS.)

It's not worth it to wish for spellbooks of genocide, because once you cast
such a high level spell, it takes such a long time to regenerate your
spell points.  It sure ain't worth it in hell.

Lightening bolt is a fun spell to have.  It's barely worth it in spell points.
Like having an infinite emergency wand of lightening.  (Use sparingly.)

Now, for us far and away the best spellbook is:

                           Magic Mapping!

Very, very, very, useful in hell.  I waste wishes on it nowadays...

